I'm so excited.

155
00:08:14.940 --> 00:08:20.140
Season two, episode 22, written by Steve Carell.

156
00:08:20.400 --> 00:08:21.420
Steve Carell.

157
00:08:21.680 --> 00:08:21.980
All right.

158
00:08:22.140 --> 00:08:24.740
Written by Steve Carell, directed by Ken Quapas.

159
00:08:24.900 --> 00:08:27.140
This is already off to a good start, Jenna.

160
00:08:27.140 --> 00:08:28.160
Oh, yeah.

161
00:08:28.340 --> 00:08:29.480
This is a good team.

162
00:08:29.860 --> 00:08:30.440
All right.

163
00:08:30.660 --> 00:08:31.720
Hit me with a summary.

164
00:08:32.040 --> 00:08:33.200
Here we go.

165
00:08:33.840 --> 00:08:39.580
Michael decides to hold a casino night down in the warehouse to benefit charity.

166
00:08:40.660 --> 00:08:45.120
However, Michael is going to have to juggle two dates.

167
00:08:45.900 --> 00:08:46.980
Bum, bum, bum.

168
00:08:46.980 --> 00:08:54.600
Um, also Jim is dealing with his possible transfer to the Stanford branch and that's going to lead

169
00:08:54.600 --> 00:08:58.020
him to ponder his relationship with Pam.

170
00:08:58.200 --> 00:08:59.080
It's a good one.

171
00:08:59.320 --> 00:09:01.160
Should we get into some Sam?

172
00:09:01.360 --> 00:09:02.420
Fast fact.

173
00:09:02.640 --> 00:09:03.040
Yeah.

174
00:09:03.840 --> 00:09:09.520
Fast, fast fact number one, written by Steve Carell.

175
00:09:10.060 --> 00:09:12.860
So here's something that maybe a lot of people don't know.

176
00:09:12.860 --> 00:09:15.240
Steve also co-wrote 40-Year-Old Virgin.

177
00:09:15.480 --> 00:09:17.900
This is not the first thing that Steve has written.

178
00:09:17.920 --> 00:09:20.060
So he got to write this episode.

179
00:09:20.200 --> 00:09:24.480
You know, he also wrote another episode of The Office, season four, episode seven, Survivorman.

180
00:09:24.900 --> 00:09:26.500
So we'll get to that one.

181
00:09:26.660 --> 00:09:33.960
But I found an old article from the LA Times where Steve did an interview all about how

182
00:09:33.960 --> 00:09:37.760
he came to be the writer of this season two finale.

183
00:09:37.760 --> 00:09:41.440
Lady, that is some good detective work.

184
00:09:41.440 --> 00:09:42.280
I love it.

185
00:09:42.400 --> 00:09:43.380
I was very proud of myself.

186
00:09:43.620 --> 00:09:43.880
All right.

187
00:09:44.020 --> 00:09:45.260
So here's how it went down.

188
00:09:45.540 --> 00:09:51.300
So Steve had the idea as a general episode that Michael should throw a casino night down

189
00:09:51.300 --> 00:09:51.940
in the warehouse.

190
00:09:52.260 --> 00:09:58.820
And he pitched that idea to Greg and Greg said, oh, that would make a great setting for

191
00:09:58.820 --> 00:10:00.080
the finale episode.

192
00:10:00.640 --> 00:10:06.480
And then I guess, remember when Steve had to fly to New York to do the Valentine's Day

193
00:10:06.480 --> 00:10:06.900
episode?

194
00:10:07.140 --> 00:10:07.300
Yeah.

195
00:10:07.760 --> 00:10:11.080
On the airplane, Steve and Greg were together.

196
00:10:11.080 --> 00:10:14.120
And on the way there and on the way back, they sat down.

197
00:10:14.300 --> 00:10:19.120
They sort of plotted out together what the finale would be with this casino night idea.

198
00:10:19.380 --> 00:10:20.180
Isn't that incredible?

199
00:10:20.520 --> 00:10:22.140
And they're outlining this episode.

200
00:10:22.380 --> 00:10:23.400
I love that.

201
00:10:23.880 --> 00:10:27.080
And Greg said to Steve, why don't you write it?

202
00:10:27.400 --> 00:10:28.340
You should write it.

203
00:10:28.520 --> 00:10:32.000
And Steve was like, OK, yeah, I mean, I think that's great.

204
00:10:32.180 --> 00:10:34.160
But then this is a really fun tidbit.

205
00:10:35.300 --> 00:10:36.620
Steve got cold feet.

206
00:10:36.620 --> 00:10:39.200
Oh, and he tried to get out of it.

207
00:10:40.020 --> 00:10:44.880
So I guess this was during the time when he was still doing Evan Almighty on the weekends.

208
00:10:45.120 --> 00:10:49.000
You know, he would shoot on our show sometimes like four days a week.

209
00:10:49.140 --> 00:10:51.620
We would shoot him out in four days and then he would fly.

210
00:10:51.780 --> 00:10:53.660
And on the weekends, he would be doing Evan Almighty.

211
00:10:53.660 --> 00:11:05.160
And so he flew home and he had one weekend to write this finale script and his parents were in town visiting him at his house.

212
00:11:05.640 --> 00:11:11.260
And so he went into Greg's office and he said, I just don't think I can do it justice.

213
00:11:11.260 --> 00:11:12.580
There's no way I can do it.

214
00:11:12.680 --> 00:11:14.000
I should get out of it.

215
00:11:14.300 --> 00:11:17.400
But Steve had this great quote where he said, I don't know.

216
00:11:17.540 --> 00:11:19.640
Greg managed to talk him into doing it anyway.

217
00:11:19.640 --> 00:11:25.080
Like Greg has a way where by the time he was leaving Greg's office, he was like, I'm writing this episode again.

218
00:11:25.200 --> 00:11:25.940
How did that happen?

219
00:11:26.100 --> 00:11:27.140
I should not get out of it.

220
00:11:28.320 --> 00:11:30.780
So that makes me smile because it's very great.

221
00:11:30.980 --> 00:11:33.760
Greg, you know, we've sort of all said that he was kind of like our dad.

222
00:11:34.300 --> 00:11:34.500
Yeah.

223
00:11:35.040 --> 00:11:37.320
Although he's not old enough to be our dad, obviously.

224
00:11:37.520 --> 00:11:41.280
But he had sort of that father mentor thing of like, you'll figure it out.

225
00:11:41.420 --> 00:11:42.160
You're going to do it.

226
00:11:42.240 --> 00:11:43.020
It's going to be OK.

227
00:11:43.180 --> 00:11:44.380
And then you're like, wait, it's going to be great.

228
00:11:44.620 --> 00:11:45.900
Wait, I can totally see that.

229
00:11:46.100 --> 00:11:47.640
I came in here to tell you I'm not doing it.

230
00:11:48.300 --> 00:11:49.940
And now I'm writing it.

231
00:11:50.200 --> 00:11:50.340
Yeah.

232
00:11:50.840 --> 00:11:52.700
But he did say he was really pumped.

233
00:11:52.940 --> 00:11:57.340
Like that was the other part of it was that Greg really infused him with this enthusiasm.

234
00:11:57.440 --> 00:11:58.660
So he was really excited.

235
00:11:59.080 --> 00:12:03.740
So what he did was he went home and he would write the script after his career.

236
00:12:03.760 --> 00:12:05.160
His parents went to bed.

237
00:12:05.580 --> 00:12:07.640
So he would stay up at night and write the script.

238
00:12:07.800 --> 00:12:08.720
And he turned it in.

239
00:12:09.040 --> 00:12:12.420
And he said then the writing staff did a bunch of punch ups.

240
00:12:12.860 --> 00:12:14.360
And like they usually do.

241
00:12:14.580 --> 00:12:14.780
Right.

242
00:12:14.920 --> 00:12:16.520
And that's how we got the script.

243
00:12:16.900 --> 00:12:17.280
Wow.

244
00:12:17.740 --> 00:12:18.760
Way to go, Steve.

245
00:12:19.140 --> 00:12:20.120
Way to like crazy.

246
00:12:20.400 --> 00:12:22.000
Man, I love that story.

